The registry also covers configuration immutability. Live policy or limit
reconfiguration is not currently a legal command; adding it would require an
atomic migration transition and reopen every evidence/resource theorem.

The registry is implemented at three proof scopes. This is a semantic scope
cut, not permission to omit an already-tested boundary:

| Proof scope | Families | Required artifact |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sequential kernel | retained admission, compute ownership, final membership, chain reconciliation and the committed effect log | executable `KernelStep_E`, state invariants and exact transition/refinement tests |
| Boundary protocol automata | Remote/Proposal known and in-flight handoff, effect delivery, template convergence, persistence/restart and shutdown | small protocol state machine, safety/temporal property and production trace refinement; the endpoint implementation itself is not reimplemented |
| Trusted external functions | consensus snapshot construction, non-contextual/contextual/VM validation, database/network internals and cryptographic primitives | exact typed input/output contract, identity/context binding and fault injection at the boundary |

Existing production tests may discharge a refinement edge, but "already
tested" or theorem novelty can never remove a family from `Legal`, `Obs`,
`Live` or `Cost`. In particular, the open pre-authority known/in-flight handoff
finding disproves any blanket claim that the relay boundary is already closed.
Template and persistence remain protocol refinements even when their inner
algorithms need not be duplicated in the sequential kernel.

M0 permits only these trusted premises; everything else is modeled as hostile
or fallible:

| Premise | Exact scope | What is not assumed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Consensus snapshot integrity | ordered chain commands contain consensus-validated blocks and the exact installed snapshot | allocation succeeds, detached recovery fits, or a proposal short ID is collision-free |
| Cryptographic identity | CKB full hashes provide protocol identity under the project's cryptographic assumption | raw hash and witness hash have the same role, or short IDs are unique |
| Validation functions | non-contextual, resolution, VM and contextual functions return sound typed evidence for their exact inputs | evidence survives another view/rules/payload identity or pool-overlay change |
| Process-monotonic time | `Instant` does not move backward within one process | time advances promptly, clocks survive restart, or a wall-clock timestamp is monotonic |
| Operational wall clock | the supported host reports a time after the Unix epoch; reads may repeat or move backward and `accepted_at` remains an exact public/policy value | expiry deadlines form a monotonic level, NTP/administrator changes are bounded, or wall time survives restart |
| Executor fairness | an enabled non-cancelled task is eventually polled, stated only for conditional liveness theorems | any latency bound under CPU starvation or a global rank for the infinite service |
| External endpoint contract | callback, relay, ban and recent-reject calls return, fail or hit their documented circuit boundary | crash durability or universal exactly-once delivery |
| Process integrity | memory safety, FFI and process survival match the explicit residual-risk boundary | recoverable OOM allocation, panic recovery or repair after memory corruption |
| Static configuration | startup constructors validate one immutable configuration before ingress opens | online reconfiguration or correctness of arbitrary unvalidated numeric combinations |

Persistence bytes, network transactions, peers, declared cycles, message
ordering, controller saturation, endpoint failure, cancellation, allocator
failure and chain timing are not trusted premises. They must have ordinary,
bounded model outcomes.

M0 also freezes the abstraction function instead of letting the reference
model invent convenient fields. For every stable production cut `p`,
`Alpha_E(p)` produces one model state and every semantic observation must
commute with extraction:

```text
ObsModel(Alpha_E(p)) = Normalize_E(ObsProduction(p))
```

`Normalize_E` may alpha-rename only the opaque tokens allowed by section 3.2.
The initial extraction registry is:

| Abstract value | Production extraction and exact meaning | Required context / forbidden shortcut |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TxIdentity = (raw, witness)` | `TransactionView::{hash,witness_hash}` through `TxIdentity::from_transaction` | raw owns pool identity; witness plus script rules owns verification-cache identity; they are never interchangeable |
| `ProposalKey` | proposal short ID derived from the raw hash | lossy lookup key only; collision candidates remain a bounded set and short ID never proves identity |
| `CellFootprint = (W,P,R,H)` | inputs and outputs from the transaction; direct deps plus resolved dep-group members from `ResolvedPayload`; header deps from the transaction | raw extraction cannot guess expanded dep-group members; pool-origin/chain-origin is evidence, not an outpoint property |
| `ChainContext` | paired `Arc<Snapshot>` and `ChainViewId`, script rules, time/DAO/since/maturity context | all values come from one named capture; same tip does not imply the same rules or pool overlay |
| `SourceContext` | Remote peer/residency, Proposal base/window, Recovery lease or owner-free Local/TestAccept command, plus arrival/deadline | source priority, expiry and fairness remain observable policy and cannot be reconstructed from the transaction |
| `ValidationEvidence` | sealed non-contextual, resolved, verification and membership receipts | exact subject, view, rules, read set and observed values; a nearby version/hash/cut is not evidence |
| `PolicyFootprint` | fee, cycles, serialized/resolved size, package order, parents/readers/spenders, RBF victims and eviction frontier | state-dependent at the final read cut; disjoint cells alone do not imply commutativity |
| `ResourceVector` | typed payload/resident/weighted/effect bytes, encoded/expanded edges, entries, cycles and active work | every conversion is checked and named; one raw `usize` cannot cross dimensions |
| `EffectFootprint` | canonical logical effects and `EffectPolicy` derived from the planned disposition | commitment belongs to Apply; endpoint delivery and acknowledgement are a separate protocol trace |
| `LinearClaim` | owner/version compute capability, effect claim or ordered command ownership | at most one mutation right; a stale claim may exist only with an empty mutation right and bounded retirement |
| `ProtocolHandoff` | controller message plus relay known/in-flight ownership and its acknowledgement state | pre-authority state is not hidden as an endpoint detail; every success/failure/cancellation settles or releases it exactly once |

Finite exploration abstracts full hashes to distinct symbolic atoms plus an
explicit short-ID collision relation. Distinct raw transaction bodies cannot
share a raw hash under the cryptographic premise, but the model must generate
same-raw/different-witness variants because CKB raw identity excludes witness
data; their proposal key is shared while verification identity differs. It
abstracts consensus and VM execution to typed evidence or typed rejection,
never to an unchecked Boolean. Small scopes enumerate identity aliases, peers,
views, resource boundaries and interleavings; algebraic conservation and graph
induction carry the result to configured count/byte/edge bounds.

### 3.2 Stable observations and equivalence

`Obs` is evaluated only at a named stable cut. An authority cut has no private
prepared delta holding the authority guard; checked-out compute capabilities
and committed effect claims may still be explicit state. A boundary-protocol
cut lies between atomic handoff/publisher/template/persistence steps, with
channel contents and acknowledgements represented rather than hidden inside a
call. A crash cut may occur between any two protocol steps and exposes only
the documented durable projection. Kernel batch equivalence uses authority
cuts; end-to-end linearizability composes authority and boundary-protocol cuts.

`Obs` contains:

- each command's committed or non-committing disposition;
- service readiness plus controller enqueue/full/closed, response and caller-
  abandonment outcomes;
- RPC owner/status/detail/pool-info/fee results;
- accepted membership, spender/reader/causal relations and resource usage;
- query, persistence, compact-block, relay-parent and template input receipts;
- source cuts and the exact ordered committed-effect stream;
- pre-authority relay-known/in-flight settlement and peer-ban outcomes where
  the cross-crate protocol makes them observable.

Endpoint execution after effect commitment is a separate delivery trace. A
kernel batch theorem stops at the committed effect log; publisher liveness and
the documented at-least-once action/acknowledgement window are protocol
properties, not silently folded into atomic Apply.

`Equivalent` is field-specific. Opaque internal tokens may be alpha-renamed
only if the renaming preserves every reference and cannot affect ordering,
validity, deadline or a public result. `ApplySequence`, chain/source cuts,
arrival order when used by policy, and committed-effect order are exact and
cannot be quotiented away. Concurrent history equivalence must preserve
real-time precedence and coherent read cuts.

The observation registry is deliberately broader than RPC:

| Observation | Equality/convergence requirement |
| --- | --- |
| Service lifecycle and controller handoff | exact bootstrapping/running/draining/stopped readiness, exact payload location and separate enqueue/result acknowledgement; caller abandonment never changes the already selected authority disposition |
| Command disposition | exact per command, including duplicate, reject, pressure, stale/cancel and callback result |
| Owner and resource reads | exact at one linearized authority read cut |
| Cell/membership/compact-block reads | exact to the same owner/membership/view cut; serialization happens after the guard opens |
| Relay known/in-flight and parent rebuild | exact handoff settlement or a named conservative reset followed by bounded rebuild |
| Committed effects | exact contents, class and canonical order; endpoint delivery is checked by the separate protocol trace |
| Recent reject record/count and fee estimate | a pending committed rejection is an exact per-hash overlay until endpoint settlement; the external TTL/sharded store may evict within its bounded policy, its total count is explicitly approximate and excludes pending log records, and disabled storage is exactly `None`; fee-estimator state is derived; no storage/arithmetic failure may alter authority or generation validity |
| Persistence/restart | exact parent-first capture of the documented owner subset; replay outcome may differ only through fresh validation under the new view |
| Template | exact source/version receipt and deterministic packing; asynchronous publication is eventual convergence, not instantaneous equality |
| Operational liveness and cost | task identity, wake hints and cache presence are semantically hidden but remain observable to `Live` and `Cost` |
| Metrics and diagnostics | semantic gauges equal their named authority/read projection at publication; lag, logging and exporter failure cannot affect dispositions; profiling-only spans preserve the default lock scope |

Public projection equivalence is API-specific; it must not infer internal
state from a deliberately collapsed field:

| Production owner/state | Transaction lookup | Pool summary / ID surfaces | Persistence and history |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `Accepted(Pending)` or `Accepted(Gap)` | RPC `Pending` | counted/listed as pending; `Gap` is intentionally collapsed | persisted in accepted parent-first order |
| `Accepted(Proposed)` | RPC `Proposed` | counted/listed as proposed | persisted in accepted parent-first order |
| `PreAccepted(Queued(Resolve))` or `Waiting` | RPC `Pending` | not an accepted pending ID; `Waiting` alone contributes orphan count | only Recovery source is persisted; it re-enters validation |
| `PreAccepted(Queued(Verify))`, verify `Computing`, or `Ready` | proposal-window-derived RPC Pending/Proposed | only queued Verify contributes verify-queue count; none is an accepted ID | only Recovery source is persisted |
| resolve or continuous `Computing` | conservatively RPC `Pending` | not an accepted ID | only Recovery source is persisted |
| `ReplacementHistory` | absent from live lookup, then recent-reject fallback | absent from pending/proposed IDs; exposed only as the legacy conflicted list | never persisted as ownership; optional bounded history may be discarded as one set |

The exact mapping above is compatibility behavior, not evidence that two
internal states are equal. RPC Pending therefore cannot be used to decide
scheduler, proposal or recovery semantics. The generated architecture
contract checks every public builder/controller/relay-receiver method and each
`Message`, `ChainControl`, owner/source/read status and committed-effect
variant against one model family. Every used family in turn names existing
behavior-registry evidence. A new public boundary, an orphan model family or a
family backed only by prose therefore cannot bypass this registry.

The single-family mapping names the semantic owner; it is not an exemption
from cross-cutting protocols. A separate generated-domain binding applies
ordinary request conservation to every discovered `Message` variant and the
reliable ordered-request law to every discovered `ChainControl` variant. The
binding stores only domain names and behavior IDs, while the checker discovers
the members from Rust. Adding a message therefore cannot silently omit its
shared handoff law or require another copied variant list.

