### 9.9 Comparator topology: self-fused workers

The coordinator-free hedge keeps the current worker set. After finishing one
execution, a worker converts the execution capability to a finished-work
capability, returns the count token to the shared fair semaphore, and may only
use a token acquired again through that semaphore. Holding both the finished
capability and the newly acquired token authorizes one atomic Apply that
settles the old owner and, when compatible work exists, checks out the next
owner for that worker. If the worker does not reacquire, or if effect capacity
blocks settlement, it settles without replacement checkout and holds no
semaphore token. This is observationally one legal adjacent
`settle; checkout` serialization and preserves the scheduler cursor and older
Local waiter order.

The hedge removes one later checkout cut per steady worker completion when the
next job is already queued, but it does not combine different workers'
settlements. The semantic exchange instead pays one serial cut per already
available bounded wave. With a fixed configured wave width both costs remain
linear in a long stream; the improvement is a mechanically counted constant
factor and scaling with worker width, not an asymptotic-complexity claim. M3.6
retains the hedge because it has no new task, channel or failure domain and can
falsify whether cross-worker exchange complexity earns its cost. It is not
allowed to satisfy T16's wave-level operation-count gate by relabeling a
per-worker Apply.

## 10. Final machine-checkable contracts

Add these invariants to the architecture contract and evidence graph before
production wiring:

- `T14 StageCommutativity`: every multi-owner Apply is either canonical-order
  equivalent or carries an exact commuting proof; the two types cannot be
  confused.
- `T15 BoundedComputeExchange`: the only retained compute checkout/settlement
  boundary consumes and produces move-only capabilities in batches bounded by
  active-work capacity; workers cannot write authority state.
- `T16 SemanticBatchProgress`: already available compatible work fills all
  available worker slots without a per-owner write round trip, and completed
  compatible work settles without waiting for a timer or slow peer.

Required falsifiers include:

1. canonical reference-model equivalence for every permutation of a bounded
   completion set and every worker completion order;
2. barrier-released W independent jobs settle in one exchange Apply and the
   next W jobs check out in that same Apply;
3. all W configured workers are simultaneously inside verification for a
   chain-backed workload;
4. a one-item workload is assigned and settled without a timer tick;
5. stale, RBF, missing-dependency, malformed-peer, effect-full and allocation
   members cannot strand or invalidate unrelated batchable members;
6. batch admission is equivalent to canonical one-item submission for owner,
   charge, effects and public outcomes;
7. the Ready result is invariant under worker completion permutations and
   matches canonical strict-priority membership;
8. operation counters fail if checkout or completion silently returns to one
   authority write per independent owner;
9. bounded-channel cancellation enumerates every schedule in the model test
   and leaves no Computing owner without a live capability;
10. post-Apply assignment delivery failure and completion-channel closure
    return and settle the exact capability without rollback or fail-stop;
11. every failed Remote request releases its exact known projection without
    waiting for unrelated batch members, and a committed prefix acknowledges
    only its matching responder prefix;
12. Proposal response arrival consumes its in-flight request once, replay is
    ignored, and only a committed Accepted effect produces known state; every
    terminal no-owner path remains refetchable without an inverse protocol;
13. the complete managed integration universe covers Remote, Proposal,
    Recovery, Local, RBF, reorg, dependency, template and ban/refetch paths.
14. a Local caller already queued on the shared compute semaphore acquires a
    permit within the next completed retained execution; no completion-carried
    permit can bypass the fair gate, while an effect-capacity-blocked
    completion retains only its original bounded worker slot and cannot block
    unrelated finished settlement.
15. if a weaker Ready member becomes stale between the two OCC reads, the
    strongest still-current prefix commits without waiting for that member;
    a stale head commits nothing, observes a changed cut and yields after one
    bounded attempt instead of synchronously spinning.
16. an unrelated Accepted insertion/removal cannot stale a direct Local or
    TestAccept negative result. A relevant producer or spender change must
    stale the exact transaction-bounded negative-evidence receipt, while a
    continuous stream of disjoint Accepted commits cannot force unbounded
    resolve/verify repetition.
17. one atomic Apply advances `ApplySequence` exactly once for batch sizes one
    through the configured bound, while every changed owner receives one
    distinct `EntryVersion`; no unobservable intermediate Apply sequence may
    be manufactured to distinguish batch members.

## 11. Safety audit that precedes implementation

The current broad `AuthorityFault` and generation-stop surfaces are not
accepted on trust. Before the exchange compiler lands, generate every
production constructor and trace backward to its external inputs. Each site
must end in exactly one class:

- construction-impossible programmer defect, with the type/owner proof and a
  negative model test;
- ordinary stale work, policy rejection, allocation/resource pressure or
  external failure, returned through a typed non-fatal boundary;
- true integrity loss whose only supported response is generation invalidation.

Any legal or hostile input reaching the third class blocks implementation and
must be removed at its producer boundary. In particular, broad conversions
from `PlanError` may not turn an unreviewed new variant into fail-stop. Public
planner methods expose operation-specific closed error enums at the producer;
downstream `from_plan` adapters may not classify an open `PlanError` family.
Projection modules remain the only normal constructors of projection faults.
Static AST checks discover constructor/boundary facts and fail on a new
constructor or conversion boundary, while semantic classification lives beside
the owning type rather than in a copied line-number allowlist.

The historical V2 pipeline is execution-topology evidence only. Its reported
single-peer secp gain came from parallel precheck/verification and handoff, but
the measurement is not a controlled current-versus-`develop` release result,
and its duplicate queues plus panic-catching failure protocol are rejected.
Only the topology lesson may influence D2; none of its safety mechanisms or
performance numbers is an acceptance premise.

Every D1 adjudication is an evidence record, not prose consensus. Its required
fields are:

| Field | Authoritative evidence |
| --- | --- |
| Semantic rule | raw/resolved/verified CKB type and protocol behavior that owns the rule |
| Production producer | AST-discovered constructor and enclosing function/module; no copied line allowlist |
| External reachability | complete caller path from controller, worker, chain, publisher, persistence or admin input |
| Impossibility or ordinary-outcome proof | sealed type/validated constructor/exact precondition, or the closed non-fatal error variant returned at the producer |
| State and resource consequence | owner, charge, projection, effect and capability before/after |
| Falsifier | negative model/unit test that would reach the bad outcome if the premise were removed |
| End-to-end anchor | production-route nextest or managed integration scenario when the boundary is externally reachable |
| Drift gate | generated selector/check that fails on a new constructor, open conversion, zero-match test or unowned rule |

An item is `PROVEN` only when all applicable fields point to exact source and
passing evidence. `SUPPORTED` means the code argument is plausible but a
falsifier or drift gate is still absent; it blocks D1 exit. `OPEN` means the
reachability or disposition is unresolved; it blocks implementation. A test
alone cannot prove constructor impossibility, and a type argument alone cannot
prove the public route preserves the intended outcome.

Task topology is part of the same table. A worker, ordered-chain or publisher
exit is not accepted as safe merely because the join layer types it: the proof
must name who owns every sender/receiver and cancellation capability, why legal
input cannot close it, and how every move-only transaction/effect capability is
returned or settled first. Derived template/cache failure remains isolated only
if it owns no lifecycle or publication capability.

## 12. Adjudication discipline

Section 8 is the frozen comparison, not permission to implement the selected
mechanism without trying to falsify it. D2 repeats the comparison after the
semantic and fault audits because either can expose a missing coupling edge.
Models may be fused only when the combined proof is smaller than the separate
protocols. For every retained or rejected model record:

- primary owner and every transient capability;
- exact state/transition algebra;
- locks, atomics, tasks, queues, channels and cancellation owner;
- batch formation and maximum size;
- single-item latency and head-of-line behavior;
- resource charging and release time;
- stale member, partial completion and worker death behavior;
- RBF/dependency/chain/effect fallback;
- asymptotic and per-transaction authority operation counts;
- new failure domains and whether risk is eliminated, bounded or transferred;
- source/test/document delta and deletion opportunity.

The selected model remains selected only while it is the smallest model
satisfying both hard gates. A contradiction reopens section 8; it does not
produce a compensating flag or exception inside section 9.

