The fault count is intentionally not an acceptance metric. The target is to
shrink semantic construction authority: domain modules own sealed projection
faults, public planner producers return closed operation-specific errors, and
the compiler forces exhaustive propagation. A generated checker then rejects
new direct constructors or open downstream classification. This removes the
manual audit surface instead of preserving 222 line-level exceptions.

The resource finding demonstrates why this evidence standard is mandatory.
The local resource arithmetic and charge ledger remain coherent; the defect is
the open error conversion at an operation boundary. `ComputeEnvelope` means
ordinary capacity exclusion for a chain recovery, but a generic `PlanError`
lets the chain boundary reinterpret it as scheduler corruption. The root
correction is not an exception at the service layer: the chain planner must
return a closed result whose ordinary recovery-exclusion/fallback variants are
unrepresentable as `AuthorityFault`, and the generated evidence gate must fail
if any open resource/planner conversion is reintroduced.

