## 21. I5 typed ordered-control freeze

I5 changes only the producer-residency boundary of the existing capacity-one
ordered lane. The production reorg publisher remains the single source already
bound to `chain/src/verify.rs`; it keeps reliable asynchronous send and never
shares a fail-fast policy with public administration. The public
administrative state is:

```text
AdminGate = Free | Held(AdminAdmission)
try_admin(Free) = Held(unique move-only capability)
try_admin(Held) = ordinary fail-fast rejection
```

One `Arc<AtomicBool>` is shared by every cloneable controller handle, but the
successful `AdminAdmission` is neither Clone nor Copy. The capability moves
with `ClearPool` or `ClearPipeline` through the existing channel and remains
owned by the ordered driver through the complete administrative operation. The
driver releases it after the operation settles and before publishing the
response, so a sequential follow-up cannot observe a stale busy gate. Channel
close, send failure, driver cancellation and operation failure all drop the
same capability exactly once.

The complete external producer term is therefore at most one trusted reorg
publisher plus one admitted public administration. Channel queue/consumer
ownership remains separately bounded by `CHAIN_CONTROL_CHANNEL_SIZE + 1`.
Excess clear calls fail before constructing a request or entering reliable
send; the caller already owns its argument, but the tx-pool boundary retains
no extra payload. No new channel, task, actor, retry, timer, lock or authority
is admissible. Ordinary ingress, reads, Direct work and all five template lanes
remain outside this protocol.

I5 executes as three cuts: (a) sealed capability and admitted command type,
(b) controller acquisition plus driver release-before-response wiring, and
(c) exact concurrency/closure regressions, generated sole-constructor/source
checks and whole-architecture convergence. Existing allocation retry inside an
already admitted administration remains the separately registered
`D1-ALLOCATION-LIVENESS` obligation; I5 neither hides nor expands it.

